      "text": "Let's label the points to make things easier:\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.3 cm);\n\npair A, B, C;\npair M, N;\n\nA = (0,0);\nB = (20,0);\nC = (10,17);\nM = (10,0);\nN = extension(A, M, B, C);\n\ndraw(A--B--C--cycle);\ndraw(A--(10 + 0.618*sqrt(5),0));\ndraw(N--M);\n\n// Labeling the points\nlabel(\"$A$\", A, NW);\nlabel(\"$B$\", B, SW);\nlabel(\"$C$\", C, SE);\nlabel(\"$D$\", M, S);\nlabel(\"$E$\", N, NE);\n[/asy]\nFrom the diagram we know that $AD$ is a median and is also trisected by the $\\text{Incircle}$ of $\\triangle ABC$. The midpoint of $AD$ is $M$. Thus, the $\\text{segment}$ $ME$ is a mean proportional between $AD$ and $EM$. Since $AD = 24$, $EM = 9$. \n\nWe want to work on finding some lengths involving the segments $AB = 20$, $CA = 16$, and $BC = 24$. Hence, using Heron's formula yields the area of the triangle:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\triangle ABC &\\triangle (\\frac{a}{2})^2\\frac{(a+b+c)}{2} = \\\\&a\\cdot h_s = \\frac{\\sqrt{400+800-24^2}}{2\\cdot a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{largest inscribed pentagon}}{2 (ab+ac+bc)} = \\\\&\\frac{9 \\sqrt{125}}{4}.\\end{align*}",
